# Flexible Humidity Sensitive Fiber with Swellable Metal–Organic Frameworks

Sijia $\mathsf{L}\mathsf{v}^{1}$ $\cdot$ Luyizheng Shuai1 $\cdot$ Wenfeng Ding1 $\cdot\cdot$ Weiquan Ke1 $\cdot\cdot$ Bing Wang1 $\cdot$ Junmin Wan1
Abstract
Humidity sensors have become an essential need for improving daily life quality. Here, a novel humidity sensitive fiber was fabricated using the soft polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) as the core layer and the non-swellable polyvinylidenedifluoride (PVDF) embedded with a swellable MIL-88A as the outer layer. Noticeably, the PDMS fiber was chosen as a carrier due to its smoothness, softness and good tensile properties. The isoreticular $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ dicarboxylate MIL-88 family with swellable metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) can undergo reversible dynamic structural transformations with a response to external humidity change. The resulted fiber with swellable MOFs showed three kinds of deformation for a single solvent and also had good deformation performance for two-component miscible solution with different volume ratios. As a proof of concept, a shape-memory effect at relative humidity from 10 to $90\%$ and the simulated salt solutions instead of relative humidity changes were used to evaluate humidity change. In addition, a deep insight into the self-shape-change mechanism among those phenomena was investigated, wherein expansion deformation of the PDMS fiber as well as the structural transformation of the MIL-88A worked in different conditions.
Keywords  Humidity sensitive $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ MIL-88A $\cdot\cdot$ MOFs $\cdot\cdot$ PDMS fiber $\cdot\cdot$ Shape-memory effect
## Introduction
For the sake of comfortable environment, humidity sensors have broad application prospects in daily life, and also have been widely used in industrial production, process control, environmental monitoring, household appliances and meteorology [1–4]. To date, the one-dimensional humidity fiber is gradually becoming a research hotspot, which provides a new way to fabricate the flexible moisture sensor. Compared with the 2D structure, the flexible 1D fiber sensors take advantages of softness, flexibility, knittability and space-saving, which will expand its application scope without reducing its humidity sensitivity to some extent [3]. Moisture sensitive materials as a core component of humidity sensors have been widely spotlighted by scientists, and can be roughly divided into three categories, ceramic (e.g., $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ), polymers (e.g., polyelectrolytes and conducting polymers) and semiconductors (e.g., GaN, $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{nO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ ) [5–7]. Among these materials, the swellable MOFs known as soft porous crystals have shown great prospects for humidity sensing [8, 9].
In the past decade, the structural transformation of the MOFs has been widely concerned. For instance, the isoreticular $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ dicarboxylate MIL-88 family that first created by Christian Serre’s group $\mathbf{MIL}=]$ Materials from Institute Lavoisier), demonstrate that the MOF unit-cell volume reversibly increases after absorbing the polar solvent molecule due to the hydrogen bond interaction between the guest molecule and the skeleton [8, 10, 11]. Furthermore, Daniel Maspoch’s group realized swellable MOF-based 2D or 3D architectures had shown a good application prospect [12, 13]. It is noted that the sensitive responsiveness stems from the reversibility of the coordination interactions within the polar solvent and the MOFs. The polarity of the solvent and the attached functional groups can effectively modulate the swelling behavior of the MOFs. With the change of the molecular level, the lattice expansion eventually leads to the macroscopic deformation of the crystal [12].
Herein, the aim of this study is to fabricate a humidity sensitive fiber, called MIL-88A@PVDF/PDMS fiber.
The soft polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) was used as the core layer and the non-swellable polyvinylidenedifluoride (PVDF) embedded with a swellable MIL-88A as the outer layer. The MIL-88A known as flexible MOFs can undergo reversible dynamic structural transformations in response to external humidity stimuli. As the PDMS fiber with softness, stretchability and lightweight properties, it is considered to apply the moisture sensitive materials. To intuitively evaluate the individual expansion property of MIL-88A crystals in the form of fiber, the absorption/desorption process between 10 and $90\%$ humidity degree is tracked. Moreover, the simulated salt solutions instead of relative humidity changes are used to further characterize the fiber deformation. We also explore different shape changes of the fiber immersed in different organic solutions in order to discuss the synergistic effect of structural transformation of MIL88A and the swelling deformation of the PDMS fiber. And the self-shape-change mechanism in environmental humidity changes and various organic solvents should be respectively confirmed in this study.
## Results and Discussion
The schematic diagram of the fabrication of the MIL- $88\mathrm{A}@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber was presented in Fig. 1. It was a simple wet-spinning and outer coating process. The PDMS fiber was served as a carrier and a tiny one-dimensional device, while the coating MIL-88A $@$ PVDF played a paramount role in humidity sensitivity [14–16]. When environmental humidity was increased, the MIL-88A crystals swelled in both directions as accompanied by deformation of the fiber.
The actual fiber morphology can be obtained from the optical microscope in Fig. 2a, b at a magnification of 100 times, which clearly presented a layered structure. Nevertheless, it can also be seen the clear dividing line of the layered structure from the SEM images of the cross-section in Fig. 2a–c. There were a few large MIL-88A crystals that scattered in the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF composite. The crystals agglomerated distributed broadly on the fiber surface, which were more like a cubic morphology. Notably, the uneven size and distribution can both affect the dynamic movement of fiber. Conversely, as the morphology of the MIL-88A powder in Fig. 2g–h, the MIL-88A particles depicted similar to three-dimensional diamond structure with obvious variations in the uniformity in particle size, between 0.5 and $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ [17]. The different morphology usually showed in the fabrication process of MIL-88A according to the previous literature [13, 18]. Therefore, there should be the preparation of the MIL$88\mathrm{A}@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber promote the transformation.
The X-ray powder diffraction (XRPD) was investigated to clarify the crystal structure (Fig. 2i). The prepared fiber and MIL-88A crystal were tested under the same atmospheric humidity environment. It was observed the same characteristic peak at $2\uptheta=10.2^{\circ}$ as ascribed to the (100) crystal plane of MIL-88A, whereas the characteristic peak intensity of MIL88A@PVDF/PDMS fiber was obviously weakened [10, 19]. In addition, there was a distinct pattern at $2\uptheta=11.8^{\circ}$ of the MIL-88A spectrum that represented (101) crystal plane [20]. It also declined at the XRPD spectrum of MIL-88A@PVDF/ PDMS fiber. Consequently, it implied that the diffraction peaks in the cubic samples of MIL-88A were not significant that embedded into the PVDF composite in the form of MIL-88A $@$ PVDF outer layer [18, 21, 22]. The domination by (100) and (101) crystal faces in the outer layer of the fiber was lower than that in the MIL-88A crystals. It can also be confirmed by the selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern images in Fig. 2k–l. Apparently, the two kind morphology of diamond-like structure and cubic-like structure were both polycrystalline, which composed of (100) and (101) crystal planes with different ratios[23–25]. The cubic-like structure shown a more obvious diffraction ring centered on the transmission spot and preferred to have many other types of crystal faces. It probably caused the morphology changes on the surface of the fiber.
In Fig. 3a–b, we exposed the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber in different levels of relative humidity with absorption and desorption [26–28]. Using the red dot as the observation point, the angle change of the left and right fibers relative to the red baseline can directly observed the structural change of the MIL-88A affected by humidity. The motion process showed that the fiber gradually stretched during absorption from 10 to $90\%$ humidity, which was in good accordance with the swelling properties of MIL-88A [13]. Notably, the direction of fiber movement was largely related to the uniformity of the surface modification. A large amount of experimental data in this study demonstrated that the
MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber should gradually stretch as the humidity increasing. Correspondingly, it slightly shrank during desorption from 90 to $10\%$ , although the relative degree of swelling was so weak to return to the initial state. In fact, it was difficult to completely remove the water vapor inside the MIL-88A crystals during the desorption process if had not enough dried in $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven. Simultaneously, we used the simulated salt solution instead of relative humidity response to examine its sensitivity and repeatability in Fig. 3c. According to the relative humidity table of the saturated salt solution, the saturated LiCl, $\mathrm{\mathbf{Mg}C l}_{2}$ , NaBr, $\mathrm{\DeltaNaCl}$ , KBr and ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution represent $11\%$ , $33\%$ , $59\%$ , $75\%$ , $81\%$ and $97\%$ humidity, respectively [29, 30]. Compared with similar moisture in the adsorption process, most of salt solution shown a general consistent deformation characteristics. On the other hand, the results also well supported the shape-memory property of the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS humidity sensitive fiber.
Otherwise, the sensitivity of the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/ PDMS fiber soaking in various organic solvents [12] was evaluated in Fig. 4a. Deformation in a single solvent can be divided into three types: stretch in water, ethanol and acetonitrile; bend after stretching in acetone; irregular involution in toluene and trichloromethane. It can return to its original state of every deformation after being dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for several minutes or placed in a room temperature environment within a long-term of time. In addition, the structure change of MIL-88A crystals played an important role in the deformation in water, ethanol, acetonitrile and acetone solutions. The stretching degree was in regular pattern, which was relevant with the functional group and polarity of the solvent in the form of hydrogen bond [12, 31]. To ascertain the contention, we experimented the reversible conversion of the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber in above solutions in a certain order (Fig. 4b). The angle of bending gradually increased when the fiber was immersed from water, ethanol, acetonitrile to acetone, and it also worked in reverse process. The determination of the bending fulcrum was owing to the inhomogeneous MIL-88A@PVDF surface. Impressively, the deformation in the acetone solution undergone a process of first recovery and then stretching, which was manifested as the angle increased first and then decreased. It was considered to be the micro swelling properties of PDMS in acetone, while it had almost no impact in water, ethanol and acetonitrile [32]. The deformation and expansion of the microstructure inside the polymer chain can cause the macroscopic fiber to move [33, 34]. The deformation behavior can be described into two steps: the coated MIL-88A works in the first few seconds; when acetone penetrates into the PDMS fiber, the expansion deformation of the PDMS fiber in the presence of acetone as well as the structural deformation of the MIL-88A would cause the fiber bending. Therefore, the shape deformation showed the results from synergistic effect of the PDMS fiber and the MIL-88A, although the swelling properties of the PDMS only accounted for a little.
Meanwhile, as for toluene and trichloromethane, previous literatures had mentioned the inertia of MIL-88A in them [12]. That was to say that the strong involution of the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber had nothing to do with crystal structure deformation of the MIL-88A. Thus, we speculated that PDMS network appeared large-scale swelling causing distortion of the microstructure in toluene and trichloromethane [35, 36]. It mainly caused microchannel deformation in the third dimension, whereas the mechanical properties became gradually brittle [37]. As can be seen in Fig. 5b, the tensile stress—tensile strain curve of the MIL88A@PVDF/PDMS fiber immersing in various organic solvents indicated the significant deterioration of mechanical properties in acetone, toluene and trichloromethane. Concurrently, in view of the hydrophobicity of PDMS, it can still maintain the original high elasticity and toughness in water. As PDMS fiber in ethanol and acetonitrile, the impact of organic solvents was so minimal that it can be ignored [38]. On the whole, this law conformed to the above conjecture to a large extent.
However, it should be noted that pure PDMS fiber did not deform because of the mutual cancellation of the forces. The MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber in Fig. 4a showed irregular involution as a result of the uneven thickness of surface structure. In this case, different absorption rate of the solution caused different swelling rate of the PDMS fiber, which endowed the anisotropic at each point. To the end, it should be concluded that the uniformity of surface coating, sensitivity to MIL-88A lattice changes and swelling characteristics of PDMS in organic solvents all effect the structure change in different conditions.
The Fig. 5a was the thermogravimetric (TG) curve, the weight loss was roughly divided into three stages: the first stage of weight loss was at $250–400\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ , and the weight loss was $5\%$ ; the second stage of weightlessness was at $400{-}600\ {\mathrm{~\textperthousand}}$ , and the weight loss was $37\%$ ; the third stage of weightlessness was at $600{-}800\ \mathrm{^circC}$ , and the weight loss was $76\%$ . The fastest weight loss rate was at around $520^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . According to the records, the main occurrence of the PDMS in heat treatment process was the removal reaction of side chain ${\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ with the temperature from 250 to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [39, 40]. The relative molecular mass of - ${\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ in the PDMS structure was roughly calculated to account for $36\mathrm{-}41\%$ [41, 42]. Indeed, the PDMS played a dominant role in whole stage of weight loss, and accompanied by the HF loss of the PVDF. When the temperature rose up to $480^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the substance would decompose HF gas, which also explained why it was a sharp loss of quality at around $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . To this end, it can be seen that it had good thermal stability.
Meanwhile, there were many factors that affected the resulted fiber stretching. As seen in Fig. 5c, d, compared with pure PDMS fiber, the tensile stress and the load of the MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber both decayed under the same tensile strain. In general, it was ascribed to the step of the coated fiber placed into the oven at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . High temperature facilitated the polymer chain to spontaneously move in the direction of entropy increase, which leaded to lower molecular chain orientation [43]. The maximum load of the fiber breaks was about $0.44\mathrm{N}$ with the tensile stress at about $3.5\mathrm{MPa}$ , which was accounted for half of the pure PDMS fiber, respectively. However, MIL- $88\mathrm{A}@\mathrm{PVDF}/$ PDMS fiber displayed higher elongation at break to about $250\%$ , while PDMS fiber only to about $175\%$ . On the whole, the PDMS fiber still maintained well stretching properties after modified.
Subsequently, to explore the deformation of the MIL88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber in different component solutions, repetitive experiments were carried out in two-component miscible solution with different volume ratios of the ethanol-acetone, toluene-ethanol and acetone-toluene. As the ethanol-acetone solution exhibited in Fig. 6a, the morphology gradually changed from involution to extending with the volume ratio of the ethanol increasing, which eventually tended to be similar to that of pure ethanol solution. The distance between the ends of the fiber was chosen to express the degree of bending and the curve was basically rising in Fig. 6a. In the toluene-ethanol and acetone-toluene mixed solution (Fig. 6b, c), the curl stretched with the volume ratio of ethanol and acetone increasing, respectively. It should be noted that the fiber bended after stretched in the absence of acetone. Hence, the results substantiated that the solution with a larger volume fraction played a decisive role in deformation. It exhibited the same situation when came to the ethanol solution with different water ratio. The larger proportion of the ethanol solution leaded to the shorter time of the fiber completely stretching, as the falling curve represented in Fig. 6d. Furthermore, it should be mentioned that the resulted fiber immersed in pure ethanol can only take within $10~\mathrm{s}$ to complete deformation shape.
## Conclusion
In conclusion, we had successfully fabricated a humidity sensitive fiber called MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber, and the PDMS fiber was loaded with a swellable MIL-88A guest embedded in a non-swellable PVDF host as a fiber coating.
Noticeably, the isoreticular $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ dicarboxylate MIL-88 family with swellable MOFs can undergo reversible dynamic structural transformations in response to external humidity change. The PDMS fiber with a wet-spinning method had excellent properties such as smoothness, softness and good tensile properties, which had not weakened after being modified. Meanwhile, we had explored the deformation characteristics and principles of the fibers in different situation. It showed a shape-memory effect at relative humidity from 10 to $90\%$ during absorption or desorption as well as in the simulated salt solution, the mechanism of which was mainly the structural transformation of the MIL-88A. The deformation change can be described as stretch in high humidity degree and contract in low one. Additionally, the fiber exhibited three kinds of deformation in organic solvents and had good deformation performance in two-component miscible solution with different volume ratios. In this regard, the expansion deformation effect of the PDMS fiber in solution should be both account for the reason. Although there was still a great deal of uncertainty, the stretching order of the fiber in water, ethanol, acetonitrile and acetone can be basically determined. In a word, the MIL- $88\mathrm{A}@\mathrm{PVDF}/$ PDMS fiber has excellent moisture sensitivity with wide application prospect.
## Experimental Section
## Materials
The Ferric chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\$ ) was purchased from Hangzhou Gaojing Fine Chemistry Industry Co., Ltd. The fumaric acid was obtained from Sinopharm Chemistry Reagent Co., Ltd. N, N-Dimethyl formamide, petroleun ether, polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) and ethanol were supplied by Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. PDMS (Sylgard-184) was purchased from Dow Corning Company. All solvents and reagents were purchased from commercial suppliers and were used without further purification. All experiments were carried out under ambient atmosphere.
## Synthesis of MIL‑88A
A solution of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{~\~}_{\mathrm{~0.540~g~}}$ , 2 mmol) and fumaric acid $\left(0.232\ \mathrm{g},2\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ in DMF $\mathrm{10~mL}$ ) was placed into a glass vial at a temperature of $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ with stirring in a water bath. Then, after the obtained solution was centrifuged, and removed the supernatant, the precipitate was washed for 2–3 times with the DMF and ethanol, respectively. As followed, it was dried at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in oven.
## Synthesis of PDMS Fiber
The main agent (part A) and the hardener (part B) of the PDMS were uniformly mixed at a mass ratio of 10:1, using a vacuum to float the bubbles in the mixture to the surface and rupture. Simultaneously, PDMS fiber was obtained by simple homemade spinning equipment with the silicone oil bath at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Next, it was washed with petroleum ether to remove the PDMS, and then washed with ethanol to remove petroleum ether, and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## Fabrication of MIL‑88A@PVDF/PDMS Fiber
The MIL-88A powder $\left(0.125\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ and the PVDF $(0.125\ \mathrm{g})$ were mixed with the DMF $\cdot1.25~\mathrm{ml},$ ). After sonication for
$30~\mathrm{min}$ , the mixture was drop coated onto the obtained PDMS fiber evenly. Next, put it in the oven and heat it to $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The resulting coated fiber was washed using the ethanol and dried at air.
## Characterization
The optical micrograph images were collected on OLYMPUS CX22 LEDRFS1. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were collected on a vltra55 scanning electron microscope with gold plating. X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns under ambient conditions were collected on ARL XTRA X-ray diffractometer. The TEM images and selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern images were collected on JE2100 transmission electron microscope. The thermogravimetric (TG) patterns were collected on PYRIS 1 thermogravimetric analyzer and the differential thermal gravity (DTG) pattern was a differential curve of TG. The tensile test patterns were collected on KFT tensile stress tester. The humidity sensitivity of the resulted fiber was tested by immersing it in different kinds of solvents. The same MIL-88A $@$ PVDF/PDMS fiber with length of $3\mathrm{cm}$ was placed in the same volume of water, ethanol, acetonitrile, acetone, toluene and chloroform. Reversible conversion of fiber shape in water, ethanol, acetonitrile and acetone solutions was also experimented. After taking it out of the solution, it was washed with the ethanol and placed in a $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, using the different volume ratio of ethanol-acetone, toluene-ethanol and acetonetoluene, as well as the saturated LiCl, $\mathrm{\mathbf{Mg}C l}_{2}$ , NaBr, $\mathrm{{NaCl}}$ , KBr and ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution, the same experiment was carried out. Finally, it was taken humidity absorption and desorption in Germany Binder KMF 115 constant temperature and humidity incubator. After being taken out from the oven, the fiber was putted in the incubator and only changed relative humidity (from 10 to $90\%$ and from 90 to $10\%$ ) maintaining the same temperature of $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
